{"kind":"request","text":"claude: make the full Go test gate dramatically faster through shared test infrastructure, without weakening any test. Hugh asked today (2026-09-01) after the full gate's slowness; measurements below are from a complete `go test -count=1 ./...` run on his machine at local main c3169d83.\n\nMEASURED. Package wall times: cmd/gs 476s, internal/app 216s, internal/kernel 206s, cmd/gitseq-mcp 166s, internal/service 86s, internal/docset 81s — five packages carry over 80% of roughly 24 CPU-minutes. The cost is per-test workroom construction over real git subprocesses: 53 git-init call sites across the test suites, and each signed append costs on the order of 170ms (the depth-64 fixture in cmd/gitseq-mcp takes ~11s alone).\n\nTHREE LEVERS, IN EXPECTED-IMPACT ORDER. (1) Disable fsync for test git processes at the one existing seam: internal/testgit.Isolate (environment.go:37) already sets process-wide hermetic git config env for every git-using test package; extend it to inject core.fsync=none, core.fsyncMethod=batch, and gc.auto=0 via the GIT_CONFIG_COUNT/KEY/VALUE mechanism it currently clears. On macOS git issues F_FULLFSYNC per object and ref write, and every signed append writes several of each, so this alone may cut per-append cost severalfold across every package for ~10 lines. It changes test-git durability only; tests that deliberately assert fsynced files (the cmd/gs outbox tests) fsync their own files directly and are unaffected — verify that claim. (2) Template-fixture reuse: build each workspace shape once per package and clone or copy the template repo per test instead of re-signing dozens of appends. (3) t.Parallel() on isolated tests in the heavy serial packages (cmd/gs, internal/app, internal/kernel, internal/service), keeping any elapsed-time-asserting test serial.\n\nSCOPE NOTE. cmd/gitseq-mcp is already covered by my open request b7a18938 to codex with the same levers; coordinate — put the shared mechanisms (testgit seam, a reusable fixture helper) where his lane can adopt them, and do not recut his package.\n\nCONDITIONS: (1) before/after wall-time measurements for the full gate and for each of the four heaviest packages, complete runs on one machine at the exact head; a material reduction with no numeric target mandated. (2) No test deleted, de-scoped, or weakened; timing assertions stay serial with unchanged bounds; durability-asserting tests shown unaffected by the fsync seam change. (3) Full gate green twice at the exact head to guard against parallelism flakiness. (4) Implement on a request/\u003cslug\u003e worktree with Rests-On per discipline, publish exact-head artifacts for internal/testgit and each heavy package whose tests changed, and request independent review citing this request.","body":{"conditions":"Material full-gate and per-package wall-time reduction shown by complete before/after runs on one machine at the exact head; no test deleted, de-scoped, or weakened; timing assertions serial with unchanged bounds and durability-asserting tests shown unaffected by the fsync seam; full gate green twice at the exact head; exact-head artifacts published for internal/testgit and each changed heavy package; independent review requested citing this request; cmd/gitseq-mcp left to request b7a18938.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}